latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Determine the grid dimensions.\n   b. Identify the non-zero shape (main shape).\n   c. Record the main shape's color, dimensions, and position.\n\n2. Determine the complementary color:\n   - If main color is 6 (magenta), complementary is 3 (green), and vice versa.\n\n3. Calculate pattern dimensions:\n   a. Determine corner element size:\n      - Use the smaller dimension of the main shape, but cap at 3 for grids <= 20x20, or 4 for larger grids.\n   b. Determine side element size:\n      - Use the larger dimension of the main shape for both width and height.\n\n4. Create the basic pattern:\n   a. Place corner elements of the main color in all four corners of the grid.\n   b. Place side elements of the complementary color between the corners.\n   c. If the grid is small (e.g., <= 16x16), create a compact cross pattern instead.\n\n5. Center the pattern:\n   - Calculate offsets to center the entire pattern in the grid.\n\n6. Incorporate the original shape:\n   a. If the original shape fits within the pattern, keep it in its original position.\n   b. If not, integrate its dimensions into the larger pattern (e.g., extend side elements).\n\n7. Add accents:\n   - If there's space in the bottom-right corner, add a small accent (1-3 cells) of the complementary color.\n\n8. Ensure symmetry:\n   - Mirror the top-left quadrant to create the other three quadrants.\n\n9. Fill remaining space:\n   a. Fill areas inside the pattern (excluding the original shape position) with the main color.\n   b. Leave all other areas black (0).\n\n10. Validate the output:\n    - Ensure the grid maintains the required dimensions.\n    - Check that the pattern is symmetrical.\n    - Verify that the original shape or its essence is preserved.\n\n11. Return the transformed grid.\n\nThis plan allows for flexibility in handling different grid sizes and input shapes while maintaining the core pattern principles observed across the examples. It should be able to handle various input scenarios and produce consistent, symmetrical outputs that incorporate elements of the original input shape."
